--- reading of this run ---

212 passed, 16 skipped.

The 16 skips are the whole of `tests_v1/`, which cannot run on Windows: `verifiers.v1`
imports `fcntl`. They are skipped *visibly* and that is deliberate. An earlier version of
`pyproject.toml` written in this same session set `testpaths = ["tests"]`, which dropped
`tests_v1` from collection altogether and turned this line into a clean-looking
"212 passed" with no mention of the v1 suite at all. That was caught by comparing against
the previous baseline and reverted; the skips are evidence, not noise.

`tests_v1` was run separately, inside the Linux image, where it does execute:

    docker run --rm -v "e:/RL:/run/desktop/mnt/host/e/RL" ... \
      trgym-v1:latest python -m pytest tests_v1/ -q
    -> 16 passed in 71.00s

The two PyTorch warnings are emitted by the *deliberately buggy* task fixtures
`m4_schedule_accumulation` and `h4_schedule_triple_defect`, whose planted defect is
precisely a scheduler/optimizer ordering error. The warning is the defect announcing
itself and is expected.

No `PytestUnknownMarkWarning` appears: `needs_docker` is registered in `pyproject.toml`,
so `--strict-markers` in CI will not turn these tests into a collection error.
